# Pull request branch workflow

## Request-time default (binding)

**Unless the user explicitly directs otherwise in the same message** that
assigns the work (examples: “push straight to `main`”, “no PR”, “hotfix to
default branch only”), **assume all work is to be completed on a pull
request:** dedicated branch off fresh `origin/main` → commits → push → open
or update a **PR** into `main` (draft is acceptable). Silence or omission is
**not** permission to bypass a PR.

## Why this rule exists

Shipping several commits directly to the default branch (`main`) bypasses
GitHub’s review surface, hides the change set behind unrelated history, and
makes it easy to miss CI on the intended diff. **Rule 15** (doc / PR / code
parity) assumes a PR exists for final-body checks — it does **not** replace
this workflow rule.

## Rule — use a PR branch for new work

1. **Scoped work:** For any **new scoped request** (feature, WO, wave, bugfix,
   refactor), do all implementation commits on a **dedicated branch** that will
   back a **pull request** into the repo default branch (here: `main`), per the
   request-time default above.
2. **Branch off current remote default:** Before the first commit,
   `git fetch origin` and create the branch from **`origin/main`** (or the
   user-specified base), not from a stale local `main`.
3. **Naming:** Follow repo convention — e.g. `cursor/<topic>`,
   `feat/<topic>`, `fix/<topic>` — consistent with sibling branches.
4. **Push + PR:** Push the branch and **open the PR** (draft is fine) as soon
   as there is a meaningful first commit, unless the environment cannot create
   PRs — then leave explicit instructions for the human to open one.

## Rule — before every commit on a PR branch

Confirm the branch is still **appropriate to receive new commits**:

1. **`git fetch origin`** (refresh refs).
2. **If a PR already exists for this branch:** confirm it is **open** and
   **not merged** (e.g. `gh pr view --state`, GitHub UI, or API). If the PR is
   **merged** or **closed**, **do not** add further commits on that branch for
   new work — open a **new** branch from current `origin/main` instead.
3. **If default branch absorbed the work without a PR:** treat prior branch as
   read-only; start a **new** branch for any follow-up.

### Merge detection without `gh`

If `origin/main` can reach this branch’s tip and the tip is already on
`origin/main`’s history as the merge result of the PR, the branch is done —
verify with `git branch -r --merged origin/main` or compare PR state on GitHub.

## Exceptions

- The user **states in the same request** that work may bypass a PR (e.g.
  direct push to `main`), or **governance override** (`docs/governance.md`)
  directs commits to `main` / another integration branch. Log material
  governance exceptions when applicable.

## Pre-commit check

- [ ] Unless the user directed otherwise **in this request**, am I on a
      **non-default** branch created from fresh `origin/main` (or declared
      base)?
- [ ] If continuing prior work on a named branch: is its PR **still open** (or
      no PR yet and work is still part of the same scoped request)?
- [ ] Have I pushed and opened (or instructed opening) a **PR** for this
      branch, except when an explicit same-request or governance exception
      applies?
